<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden Bull of 1356</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1356">1356</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Golden Bull</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golden Bull of 1356 was a decree issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> that fixed the procedure for electing the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and future emperor. It named seven prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> and confirmed the elective character of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, remaining a fundamental constitutional document until 1806.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356.jpg" alt="Golden Bull of 1356" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl IV. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goldene_Bulle_Nahaufnahme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Golden Bull of 1356</b> confirmed the electoral vote for Saxe-Wittenberg, which passed in 1423 to the Wettin margraves of Meissen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Golden Bull of 1356</b> confirmed the count palatine of the Rhine as one of the seven electors who chose the King of the Romans, with the office of arch-steward of the Empire and the right to act as imperial vicar in the Rhineland, Swabia and the lands…</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Golden Bull</b> issued by Emperor Charles IV in 1356 settled their dispute over the right to elect the King of the Romans in favour of Duke Rudolf I of Saxe-Wittenberg, making him one of the seven prince-electors and Arch-Marshal of the Empire, with the lands around Wittenberg…</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Golden Bull of 1356</b>, issued by Emperor Charles IV, codified the election of the King of the Romans by seven designated electors: the archbishops of Mainz, Trier and Cologne, the King of Bohemia, the Count Palatine of the Rhine, the Duke of Saxony and the Margrave of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Bohemia thus became the only kingdom within the Holy Roman Empire, and its ruler one of the princes who elected the King of the Romans, a right later fixed in the <b>Golden Bull of 1356</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…turned a Magyar principality into a Christian kingdom of Latin Europe, building royal counties, the union with Croatia and the noble liberties of the <b>Golden Bull</b>, and survived the Mongol invasion of 1241 before baronial power and the end of the dynasty in 1301 opened the Angevin era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
